{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>And there was no water for the congregation.</b> By way of punishment, the water was lacking because they did not eulogize her as was fitting. For concerning Moses and Aaron it says, ״וַיִּבְכּוּ אוֹתָם בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״, but here it does not say ״וַיִּבְכּוּ אוֹתָהּ״; rather it says, ״וַתָּמָת שָׁם וַתִּקָּבֵר שָׁם״ — for in the place where she died, there was her burial immediately, and she was forgotten as a dead person from the heart, and they felt nothing at all in her absence. Therefore the water was withheld from them, so that they would know retroactively that the well had been in the merit of Miriam. And our Sages of blessed memory (Mo'ed Katan 28a) said that her death was juxtaposed to the passage of the red heifer, to tell you: just as the sacrifices bring atonement, so too the death of the righteous brings atonement. And why was she juxtaposed specifically to the red heifer? For in truth it is not a sacrifice, except that we find Scripture calls it a חַטָּאת (Numbers 19:9); but the essence is this: just as the heifer comes to purge the filth of its child, so too the righteous woman, mother of all living, through her death purges the filth of her offspring." "And this juxtaposition the midrashim bring in four places: (1) the juxtaposition of the death of the sons of Aaron to Yom ha-Kippurim, which atones (Leviticus 16:1); (2) the juxtaposition of Miriam to the red heifer; (3) the juxtaposition of the death of Aaron to the priestly garments, which atone (Numbers 20:28), as our Sages of blessed memory expounded (Mo'ed Katan 28a); (4) the juxtaposition of the death of Aaron to the breaking of the tablets (Deuteronomy 10:2–6), to say that the death of the righteous is as difficult as the breaking of the tablets. For from every righteous person four kinds of benefit accrue to the people of his generation. The first is that the entire world is sustained in his merit, as our Sages of blessed memory said (Bava Batra 17a): 'The entire world is sustained for the sake of Ḥanina my son,' and so forth — as we explained in the pericope of וַיְחִי at the threshing floor of Atad, and in the pericope of אֱמוֹר on the verse ״כִּי אִם לִשְׁאֵרוֹ״; and they are like a mother nursing her child, as it is written (Tanhuma Shemot): ״שְׁנֵי שָׁדַיִךְ״ — these are Moses and Aaron, and so Rashi explained (Song of Songs 4:5). And so we find that in the merit of Miriam they had the well, which sufficed for Israel for all their needs; and this is the juxtaposition of her death to the red heifer, the mother of the calf, which comes to set right the corruption of her child. The second is that the righteous person teaches them to walk in the way of YHWH and in mitzvah; therefore his passing is likened to the breaking of the tablets. The third is that his merit shields like a shade and like a garment made to protect from cold and heat, and when he dies their shade is removed, and behold they are stripped bare of every covering — in the manner that it says (Numbers 20:29): ״וַיִּרְאוּ כָּל הָעֵדָה כִּי גָוַע אַהֲרֹן״, and our Sages of blessed memory expounded (Rosh ha-Shanah 3a): ״וַיִּרְאוּ״ — read the yod in ״בְּצֵרֵי״, that they were seen, that is, through the removal of the cloud, which is likened to a garment; and this is the juxtaposition to the priestly garments. The fourth is that he atones for them through his death — this is the juxtaposition to Yom ha-Kippurim, and it is easy to understand."], :book :numbers, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {0 "The phrase ״צוֹאַת בְּנָהּ״ (filth/excrement of her child) plays on the red heifer purifying the impurity of Israel, her figurative offspring; rendered literally to preserve the earthy metaphor.", 1 "The citation of Bava Batra 17a is implicit in the abbreviation pattern ״וכו״; the well-known statement about Ḥanina ben Dosa appears there and in several other places in the Talmud."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:42:03.779910391Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}